INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (13, 48, 'Note 1', '<div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">The <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">zip()</code> function in Python <span style="background: transparent !important;"><strong style="background: transparent !important;">combines multiple iterables</strong> (like lists, tuples, or strings) element-by-element into a single iterator of tuples</span>.<span style="background: transparent !important;"> [<a style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">1</a>, <a style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">2</a>, <a style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">3</a>]</span></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">It takes the first item from each input list and pairs them together, then the second item, then the third, and so on.<span style="background: transparent !important;"></span></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"]&nbsp;
colors = ["red", "yellow", "pink"]&nbsp;
for fruit, color in zip(fruits, colors):&nbsp;
&nbsp; &nbsp;print(f"A {fruit} is {color}")&nbsp;
# Outputs:&nbsp;
# A apple is red&nbsp;
# A banana is yellow&nbsp;
# A cherry is pink</code></pre><div style="background: transparent !important;">Handling Unequal List Lengths</div><div style="background: transparent !important;">If the input lists are not the same length, <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">zip()</code> <strong style="background: transparent !important;">stops as soon as the shortest list runs out</strong> of items. Extra items in the longer lists are ignored.</div><div style="background: transparent !important;"><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>list_a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]&nbsp;
list_b = ["A", "B"]&nbsp;
&nbsp;print(list(zip(list_a, list_b)))&nbsp;
# Outputs: [(1, ''A''), (2, ''B'')]</code></pre><div style="background: transparent !important;">You can pass a zipped object directly into the <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">dict()</code> constructor to map one list as keys and the other as values.<span style="background: transparent !important;"> [<a style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">1</a>]</span></div><div style="background: transparent !important;"></div><div style="background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;">python</div><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>keys = ["username", "password", "role"]
values = ["admin", "secret123", "moderator"]

user_dict = dict(zip(keys, values))
print(user_dict)
# Outputs: {''username'': ''admin'', ''password'': ''secret123'', ''role'': ''moderator''}</code></pre><p><br></p></div></div></div></div></div></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"></div><span style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"></span><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"></div>', '2026-07-21 11:50:10.072', '2026-08-13 11:30:43.017');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (10, 45, 'Types| Triggers', 'https://medium.com/@smrutid12/background-jobs-in-system-design-a-technical-perspective-6c21d3d9c8c6
Use this link to learn resilient system design patterns with the help of Background jobs<div>----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</div><div><div style="color: white;"><span style="font-weight: bold; text-decoration-line: underline;">Types of background jobs</span></div><p style="color: white;"><span style="font-style: italic;">Background jobs typically include one or more of the following types of jobs:</span></p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p>-&gt;CPU-intensive jobs, like mathematical calculations or structural model analysis.</p></li><li><p>-&gt;Input and output (I/O)-intensive jobs, like a series of storage transactions or file indexing.</p></li><li><p>-&gt;Batch jobs, like nightly data updates or scheduled processing.</p></li><li><p>-&gt;Long-running workflows, like order fulfillment, or provisioning services and systems.</p></li><li><p>-&gt;Sensitive-data processing in which you send the task to a more secure location for processing. For example, you might not want to process sensitive data within a web app. You might instead use a pattern like the&nbsp;Gatekeeper pattern&nbsp;to transfer the data to an isolated background process that can access protected storage.</p></li></ul><div style="color: white;"><span style="font-style: italic;"><br></span></div><div style="color: white;"><span style="font-style: italic;">Triggers</span></div><p style="color: white;">You can initiate background jobs in several ways. They fall into one of the following categories:</p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>1.Event-driven triggers:</strong>&nbsp;An event, typically a user action or a step in a workflow, starts the task.</p></li><li><p><strong>2.Schedule-driven triggers:</strong>&nbsp;A timer invokes the task on a recurring schedule or as a single invocation at a specified time.</p></li></ul><div style="color: white;">------------------------</div><div style="color: white;">Event-driven triggers   |</div><div style="color: white;">------------------------</div><p style="color: white;">Event-driven invocation uses a trigger to start the background task. Examples of event-driven triggers include:</p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p>The UI or another job places a message in a queue. The message contains data about an action that occurred, like the user placing an order. The background task listens on this queue for new messages. It reads a message and uses the data as the input to the background job. This pattern is known as&nbsp;<em>asynchronous message-based communication</em>. If the request volume is bursty, use the&nbsp;Queue-Based Load Leveling pattern&nbsp;so that the queue buffers demand and the background task processes work at a controlled rate.</p></li><li><p>The UI or another job saves or updates a value in storage. The background task monitors the storage and detects changes. It reads the data and uses it as the input to the background job.</p></li><li><p><br></p></li><li><p>The UI or another job makes a request to an endpoint, like an HTTPS uniform resource identifier (URI), or an API exposed as a web service. It passes the data required to complete the background task as part of the request. The endpoint or web service invokes the background task, which uses the data as its input.</p></li></ul><div>
<p style="color: white;">Typical examples of tasks suited to event-driven invocation include image processing, workflows, sending information to remote services, sending email messages, and provisioning new users in multitenant applications.</p><div style="color: white;"><br></div><div style="color: white;">-----------------------------</div><div style="color: white;">Schedule-driven triggers    |</div><div style="color: white;">-----------------------------</div><p style="color: white;">Schedule-driven invocation uses a timer to start the background task. Examples of schedule-driven triggers include:</p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p> -A timer that runs locally within the application or as part of the application''s operating system regularly invokes a background task.</p></li><li><p> -A timer that runs in a different application, like Azure Logic Apps, regularly sends a request to an API or web service. The API or web service invokes the background task.</p></li><li><p> -A separate process or application starts a timer that starts the background task once after a specified time delay, or at a specific time.</p></li></ul><p style="color: white;"><br></p><p style="color: white;">Typical examples of tasks suited to schedule-driven invocation include batch-processing routines (like updating related-products lists for users based on their recent behavior), routine data processing tasks (like updating indexes or generating accumulated results), data analysis for daily reports, data retention cleanup, and data consistency checks.</p>

</div>

</div>', '2026-07-20 09:43:48.632', '2026-07-23 09:30:41.424');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (12, 47, 'Problem and Fix', 'Problem: With time, it''s observed that mysql is not launched (port and PID not assigned) when trying to start from xampp control panel. <div><br></div><div>So the fix is to create a new data folder, paste your necessary db folders and ibdata1 folder (if php/laravel is used, basically innodbs are used so ibdata1 folder is required) from data_old.</div><div><br></div><div>Steps to fix.</div><div><br></div><div>1. Go to C:\xampp\mysql and there rename the current data folder as data_old. </div><div>2. Open cmd at C:\xampp\mysql\bin and run the command -&gt; mysql_install_db.exe --datadir=C:\xampp\mysql\data</div><div><span style="font-style: italic;">This creates anew data folder inside <span style="color: white;">C:\xampp\mysql</span></span></div>3. Copy the db files from data_old and ibdata1. Then paste in newly created data folder.<div><br></div><div>Done for the day !!!
<div>

</div></div>', '2026-07-21 04:31:34.927', '2026-07-21 04:38:01.609');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (5, 44, 'BAsics', '
<p style="color: white;">Docker is an&nbsp;OS‑level virtualization&nbsp;(or containerization) platform, which allows applications to share the&nbsp;host OS kernel&nbsp;instead of running a separate guest OS like in traditional virtualization. This design makes Docker containers&nbsp;lightweight, fast, and portable, while keeping them<b><strong>&nbsp;</strong></b>isolated&nbsp;from one another.</p><span style="color: white;">Before Docker</span><p style="color: white;">Before Docker, deploying applications across different environments was a nightmare. Differences in dependencies, library versions, and OS configurations led to the infamous “works on my machine” problem.</p>
<span style="color: white;"><strong>Containerization existed long before Docker was introduced in 2013</strong></span><strong style="color: white;">.</strong> Docker did not invent containers; instead, it popularized them by wrapping existing, complex Linux kernel isolation technologies into an easy-to-use, standardized toolset<div><br></div><div>
<div style="color: white;"><div>What Docker Changed</div></div><div style="color: white;"><div>Before 2013, setting up these technologies was a highly manual, error-prone, and specialized task. Applications still suffered from the "works on my machine" problem due to subtle host configuration differences. </div></div><div style="color: white;"><div>When Docker launched, it initially used LXC as its backend engine. However, Docker revolutionized the industry by adding: </div></div><ol style="color: white;"><div><li><strong>1.The Dockerfile:</strong> A standardized, code-based blueprint to automate environment creation.</li></div><div><li><strong>2.Layered Images:</strong> Bundling the application along with all of its dependencies into a single, immutable, portable package.</li></div><div><li><strong>3. Central Ecosystem:</strong> Introducing a unified CLI and a public registry (Docker Hub) to easily download and share containers globally

Working</li><li>----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</li><li>
How Docker Actually Works<p>To understand Docker, we have to clear up a common misconception: <strong>Docker does not actually create containers.</strong></p><p>Containers are a feature of the Linux operating system kernel. Docker is simply a highly polished, user-friendly engine that makes it incredibly easy to use those existing Linux kernel features.</p><p>Let''s break down the three technical pillars that make Docker work, look at its architecture, and walk through a practical demonstration.</p></li><span style="text-decoration-line: underline;">1. The Technicalities: The Linux Kernel Pillars</span><p>When you run a Docker container, the Docker engine talks to the Linux kernel and asks it to set up three specific things:</p>A. Namespaces (Isolation)<p>Namespaces are the walls of the container. They provide isolation so that processes inside the container cannot see or interfere with processes outside the container.</p><ul><li><p><strong>--&gt;PID Namespace:</strong> Gives the container its own isolated process tree. Inside the container, your app might think it''s running as Process ID (PID) 1. But to the host OS, it''s just a normal process with a random PID like 3491.</p></li><li><p><strong>--&gt;NET Namespace:</strong> Gives the container its own isolated network stack, IP address, and routing tables.</p></li><li><p><strong>--&gt;MNT Namespace:</strong> Gives the container its own isolated file system root (/).</p></li></ul>B. Control Groups (cgroups) (Resource Limiting)<p>If Namespaces are the walls, <em>cgroups</em> are the budget. They prevent a single container from consuming all the resources of the host machine and crashing the whole server.</p><ul><li><p>You use cgroups to say: <em>"This container is only allowed to use a maximum of 512MB of RAM and 20% of the CPU."</em> If the app tries to use more memory than allowed, the kernel (OOM killer) will step in and kill it.</p></li></ul>C. Union File System (UnionFS / OverlayFS)<p>This is Docker''s magic trick for storage. <span style="text-decoration-line: underline; font-weight: bold;">A Docker Image is not one giant file; it is a stack of multiple read-only layers.</span></p><ul><li><p><strong>Copy-on-Write (CoW):</strong> When you start a container from an image, Docker takes all those read-only layers, stacks them together to look like one unified hard drive, and adds a very thin <strong>Read/Write layer</strong> on the absolute top.</p></li><li><p>If your app wants to modify a file from a lower layer, Docker copies that file up to the top R/W layer and modifies it there. This means you can run 100 identical containers from the exact same image, and they will all share the same base memory layers, saving massive amounts of disk space.</p></li></ul><span style="text-decoration-line: underline;">2. The Docker Architecture (Client-Server Model)</span><p>Docker operates on a client-server architecture.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Docker Client (docker CLI):</strong> The command-line tool you type into (e.g., docker run, docker build).</p></li><li><p><strong>Docker Daemon (dockerd):</strong> The heavy-lifter server process running in the background. It listens for API requests from the Client and actually manages the images, containers, networks, and volumes.</p></li><li><p><strong>Docker Registry (e.g., Docker Hub):</strong> The cloud storage where images are kept (like GitHub, but for Docker images).</p></li></ol><span style="text-decoration-line: underline;">3. Demonstration: The Docker Lifecycle</span><p>Let''s look at how this works in practice. Suppose you wrote a simple Python web server called app.py.</p>Step 1: The Blueprint (Dockerfile)<p>You write a set of instructions in a file named Dockerfile.</p><code class="bg-slate-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded border border-slate-700 font-mono text-xs text-rose-400"># Layer 1: Start with a minimal Linux OS and Python installed FROM python:3.9-slim # Layer 2: Set the working directory inside the container WORKDIR /app # Layer 3: Copy your code from your laptop into the container COPY app.py /app/ # Layer 4: Define what command runs when the container starts CMD ["python", "app.py"]&nbsp;</code><br><li>&nbsp;Step 2: Building the Image (docker build)<p>When you run docker build -t my-python-app ., the Docker Daemon reads the Dockerfile line by line.</p><ul><li><p>For <strong>every single line</strong>, it creates a new, read-only "layer" and caches it.</p></li><li><p>If you change app.py tomorrow and rebuild, Docker is smart enough to instantly reuse Layers 1 and 2 from its cache, and only rebuild Layer 3. This makes builds lightning fast.</p></li></ul></li>Step 3: Running the Container (docker run)<p>When you run docker run my-python-app, here is what technically happens in milliseconds:</p><ol><li><p>The Daemon pulls the my-python-app image layers.</p></li><li><p>It creates a new <strong>MNT Namespace</strong> and mounts the UnionFS (the read-only layers + a new top R/W layer).</p></li><li><p>It creates a new <strong>NET Namespace</strong> and assigns the container a private IP address.</p></li><li><p>It applies any <strong>cgroups</strong> limits you specified (e.g., --memory="256m").</p></li><li><p>It creates a new <strong>PID Namespace</strong> and executes the CMD (python app.py) as PID 1 inside that isolated environment.</p></li><li><p><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">Your application is now running securely, unaware that it is sharing the host''s Linux kernel with dozens of other containers!</span></p></li></ol></div></ol><div><font color="#ffffff" face="Inter, sans-serif"><br></font></div><div><font color="#ffffff" face="Inter, sans-serif"><br></font></div><ol style="color: white;"><div><span style="font-style: italic; text-decoration-line: underline; font-weight: bold;">BONUS TIP</span></div></ol><span style="font-style: italic; color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">If docker is all about utilizing linux kernel, then how does it work for other os like windows or darwin.</span><br><span style="color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; font-style: italic;">1. Docker on Windows (The Modern Way: WSL 2)</span><p style="color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;"><ol><li><span style="font-style: italic;">Historically, Docker for Windows had to spin up a clunky, hidden Hyper-V Virtual Machine running a tiny Linux OS just to host the containers.</span></li><li><span style="font-style: italic;">Today, it uses <b>WSL 2 (Windows Subsystem for Linux 2)</b>. Microsoft essentially built a highly optimized, lightweight, real Linux kernel directly into Windows 10/11. When you run Docker Desktop on Windows, it natively uses this WSL 2 Linux kernel. Your Windows machine is literally running a real Linux kernel alongside the Windows kernel, and Docker uses that to create the namespaces and cgroups.</span></li></ol></p><font color="#ffffff"><i><br></i></font><span style="font-style: italic; color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">2. Docker on Windows (Windows Containers)</span><br><span style="font-style: italic; color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">There is a lesser-known alternative: Windows actually built its own version of containerization into the Windows Server kernel (using Windows silos and job objects). You can run "Windows Containers" that contain IIS or .NET framework apps. However, 99% of developers use Docker on Windows to run Linux containers via WSL 2.</span><br><font color="#ffffff"><i><br></i></font><span style="font-style: italic; color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">3. Docker on macOS (The Hidden VM)</span><br><span style="font-style: italic; color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">macOS (Darwin) is based on Unix, but it is </span><b style="font-style: italic; color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">not</b><span style="font-style: italic; color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;"> Linux, and it does not have Linux namespaces or cgroups.</span><br><span style="font-style: italic; color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">To run Docker on a Mac, Docker Desktop secretly runs a lightweight, hyper-optimized Linux Virtual Machine in the background (using Apple''s Hypervisor framework). When you type docker run on your Mac terminal, the Docker Client on macOS talks over a socket to the Docker Daemon running inside that hidden Linux VM, and the container is actually created inside the VM.</span><br><b style="font-style: italic; color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">In short:</b><span style="font-style: italic; color: white; font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;"> Docker containers always require a Linux kernel. If you aren''t running Linux natively, Docker is quietly using a hypervisor or a subsystem to run a Linux kernel for you in the background.</span></div>', '2026-07-19 06:18:01.144', '2026-08-04 11:28:18.496');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (15, 50, 'PLAN', '
<span style="color: white;">Estimated Time: 2.5 to 3 Weeks (Starting August 1st)</span><p style="color: white;">Assuming you dedicate <strong>1.5 to 2 hours per day</strong> (as outlined in your 8-week Applied AI roadmap), you will have a fully functional, resume-ready project completed between <strong>August 18th and August 21st</strong>.</p><p style="color: white;">Here is the realistic breakdown of how you will spend that time:</p><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Week 1: Foundation &amp; The "Happy Path" (Aug 1 - Aug 7)</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>Days 1-2:</strong> Set up a free PostgreSQL database on Supabase or Neon. Use ChatGPT to generate SQL scripts to create e-commerce tables (users, orders, refunds) and insert 100 rows of fake data.</p></li><li><p><strong>Days 3-5:</strong> Set up your Python FastAPI backend. Write the initial prompt that sends the user''s question + your database schema to the OpenAI/Gemini API and gets a raw SQL string back.</p></li><li><p><strong>Weekend:</strong> Execute that SQL string on your database and print the results to your terminal. (At this point, you have the basic "influencer" project done).</p></li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Week 2: The Agentic Engineering (Aug 8 - Aug 14)</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>Days 1-3:</strong> Build the <strong>Self-Healing Loop</strong>. Wrap your database execution in a try/catch block.</p></li><li><p><strong>Days 4-5:</strong> Write the logic so that if the catch block triggers (e.g., "Column does not exist"), it sends the exact error back to the LLM and asks for a corrected query. Implement a "Max Retries = 3" limit so it doesn''t loop forever.</p></li><li><p><strong><br></strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Weekend:</strong> Build Phase 4 of the blueprint: Pass the raw JSON data rows back to the LLM to format them into a nice, human-readable sentence.</p></li></ul><span style="color: white;">Week 3: Optimization &amp; Presentation (Aug 15 - Aug 21)</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>Days 1-2:</strong> Set up a free Upstash Redis database. Add the Semantic Caching layer to your FastAPI backend.</p></li><li><p><strong>Days 3-4:</strong> Build a dead-simple frontend using Streamlit (Python) so you can actually type into a search bar and see the results on a webpage.</p></li><li><p><strong>Weekend:</strong> Push the code to GitHub. Write a massive, highly detailed README file with architecture diagrams (using Mermaid.js or Excalidraw) and screenshots. <strong>(Do not skip this—recruiters look at the README, not the code).</strong></p></li></ul>

', '2026-07-22 20:28:48.283', '2026-07-22 20:29:27.345');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (16, 50, 'Blueprint', '<span style="color: white; text-decoration-line: underline; font-weight: bold;">Project Blueprint: QueryLens AI</span><p style="color: white;"><strong>Subtitle:</strong> Agentic SQL Analytics Engine with Self-Healing Execution</p><span style="color: white;">The Business Domain (The "Cover Story")</span><p style="color: white;">Do not build a generic "Employee Database." Build an <strong>E-commerce Revenue Database</strong>. It sounds much more professional.
Your database will have tables like: users, subscriptions, payments, and refunds.</p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><em>Example User Question:</em> "What was our total refund amount for canceled pro-tier subscriptions last month?"</p></li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div><span style="font-weight: bold;">The "Unique" Enhancements (Why it''s resume gold)</span></span><p style="color: white;"><strong>1. The Self-Healing Execution Loop (The Agentic Part)</strong>
Standard tutorials just send the LLM''s SQL to the database. If the LLM hallucinates a column name, the app crashes.</p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><em>Your Twist:</em> You will build a try/catch loop. If PostgreSQL throws a Syntax Error, your backend catches that exact error message and sends it <em>back</em> to the LLM with the prompt: <em>"Your previous query failed with this Postgres error: [Error]. Here is the schema again. Fix the SQL and return it."</em></p></li><li><p><em>Why it matters:</em> This proves you understand backend exception handling and the reality of AI hallucinations.</p></li></ul><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>2. Semantic Caching (System Design Bonus)</strong></p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><em>Your Twist:</em> Before asking the expensive LLM, your backend checks a <strong>Redis</strong> cache. If someone asked the exact same question in the last 5 minutes, it serves the data instantly without calling the AI.</p></li><li><p><em>Why it matters:</em> It proves you understand caching and backend performance optimization, a massive plus for 1 YOE SDE roles.</p></li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div><div><span style="color: white;">----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</span></div><span style="font-weight: bold;">The Tech Stack</span></span><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>Frontend:</strong> Streamlit (Python) or a simple HTML/JS page (keep this minimal, your focus is backend).</p></li><li><p><strong>Backend:</strong> FastAPI (Python) or Node.js (Express).</p></li><li><p><strong>Database (Relational):</strong> PostgreSQL (Hosted for free on Supabase or Neon).</p></li><li><p><strong>Cache:</strong> Redis (Hosted for free on Upstash).</p></li><li><p><strong>AI Provider:</strong> OpenAI or Google Gemini API.</p></li></ul><span style="color: white;">The Architectural Flow (Step-by-Step)</span><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>Phase 1: The Request</strong></p><ol style="color: white;"><li><p>User types: <em>"Show me the top 3 users by total spend."</em></p></li><li><p>Backend checks Redis: Does this exact string exist? If yes, return cached data. If no, proceed.</p></li></ol><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>Phase 2: The Agentic Generation</strong>
3. Backend sends the user''s text + your Database Schema to the LLM.
4. LLM returns a raw SQL string.</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>Phase 3: The Execution &amp; Self-Healing</strong>
5. Backend attempts to execute the SQL on PostgreSQL.
6. <strong>[If Error]:</strong> Backend catches the Postgres error, sends the error + original prompt back to LLM to generate a fixed SQL string.
7. <strong>[If Success]:</strong> Backend receives the data rows from Postgres.</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>Phase 4: The Formatting &amp; Response</strong>
8. Backend sends the raw data rows <em>back</em> to the LLM one last time: <em>"Turn this raw JSON data into a friendly, readable sentence for the user."</em>
9. Backend saves the final answer in Redis (for 5 minutes).
10. Backend sends the final answer to the Frontend.</p><span style="color: white;">How to put this on your resume</span><p style="color: white;">Instead of "Built a chatbot," you will write:</p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><em>Architected an Agentic Text-to-SQL analytics engine using FastAPI, PostgreSQL, and LLMs.</em></p></li><li><p><em>Implemented a self-healing execution loop that catches database exceptions and prompts the LLM to auto-correct syntax errors, improving query success rate.</em></p></li><li><p><em>Integrated Redis caching to reduce LLM API latency by X% for repeated analytical queries.</em></p></li></ul>

', '2026-07-22 20:29:31.343', '2026-07-22 20:31:04.359');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (19, 52, 'Mechanics', '<div>In previous note we said, CDN is network proxy servers, so </div><div style="color: white;">A proxy server is an <strong>intermediary server</strong> that sits between a user''s device and the internet, acting as a gateway to pass requests and responses back and forth. [1, 2, 3, 4]</div><div style="color: white;">You are completely correct—a CDN is essentially a massive, globally distributed network of specialized <strong>reverse proxy servers</strong>. [1, 2, 3]</div><div style="color: white;">Here is exactly how those CDN proxy servers get and cache your data using a process called <strong>Origin Pulling</strong>. [1, 2]</div><div style="color: white;"></div><span style="color: white;"></span><div style="color: white;"></div><div style="color: white;"></div>
[ User ]  ──(1) Request File──&gt;  [ CDN Edge Server ]  ──(2) Cache Miss (Fetch)──&gt;  [ Your Origin Server ]-&gt;|
   │                                  │                                                   │                                                                                        |     
   └────────(4) Fast Delivery &lt;───────┴──────────(3) Sends File + Cache Rules &lt;───────────┘
<br><div>
<li style="color: white;"><strong>The First Request (Cache Miss):</strong> A user near London visits your site and requests an image (logo.png). The request hits the nearest London CDN edge proxy. Because the CDN does not have this file yet, it experiences a <strong>Cache Miss</strong>. [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</li><li style="color: white;"><strong>The Origin Pull:</strong> The London CDN proxy automatically forwards the request across the internet to your actual hosting server (called the <strong>Origin Server</strong>). [1]</li><li style="color: white;"><strong>The Data Transfer:</strong> Your origin server finds logo.png and sends it back to the London CDN proxy. [1]</li><li style="color: white;"><strong>Caching the Data:</strong> As the file passes through, the CDN proxy saves a copy to its local hard drive (SSD). It then delivers the file to the user. [1, 2, 3]</li><li style="color: white;"><strong>Subsequent Requests (Cache Hit):</strong> When the next 10,000 users in London ask for logo.png, the CDN proxy skips the origin server entirely. It serves the saved copy instantly from its cache</li>

<div style="color: white;">How the CDN Knows What (and How Long) to Cache</div><div style="color: white;">CDN proxies do not guess how to handle your files. They strictly follow instructions sent by your origin server via HTTP headers: [1]</div><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>1.Cache-Control Headers:</strong> Your origin server attaches tags like Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400. This explicitly tells the CDN proxy: <em>"You are allowed to cache this file, and you should keep it for 24 hours."</em> [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]</li><li><strong><br></strong></li><li><strong>2.Dynamic Content Bypass:</strong> For files that change constantly (like user bank balances or shopping carts), the origin sends Cache-Control: no-store. The CDN proxy reads this, never caches the file, and always fetches it fresh from your server. [1, 2, 3, 4]</li></ul><div style="color: white;"></div><span style="color: white;"></span><div style="color: white;"></div><div style="color: white;"><br></div><div style="color: white;"><span style="font-style: italic;">Alternative Method: Content Pushing</span></div><div style="color: white;"><span style="font-style: italic;">While <strong>Origin Pulling</strong> (fetching on demand) is used 95% of the time, some developers use <strong>Origin Pushing</strong>. This is where you manually upload your website assets directly to the CDN storage network before users even ask for them, pre-loading the global cache. [1, 2]</span></div>


</div>', '2026-07-23 18:29:09.045', '2026-07-23 18:35:54.425');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (23, 56, 'Note 1', 'Medium Content: https://sakshi8699.medium.com/system-design-application-layer-3ee07978fe4f<div>----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
<div style="color: white;"><p>I will break down the Application Layer for you, explaining exactly what it is in the OSI model, how it fits into System Design, why we separate it, and give you a simple real-world demonstration.</p><p><br></p>What is the Application Layer?<p>The Application Layer is the <b>7th and topmost layer</b> of the OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) model.</p><ul><li><p><b>The OSI Model</b> is a conceptual framework that explains how data travels from the physical hardware (like an Ethernet cable at Layer 1) all the way up to the software on your screen (Layer 7).</p></li></ul><div><br></div><ul><li><p><b>Layer 7 (Application Layer)</b> is the layer closest to the end-user. It is the interface between your network applications (like your web browser) and the network itself.</p></li></ul><p><b>Crucial Distinction:</b> The Application Layer is <i>not</i> the application itself (like Google Chrome). It is the layer of <b>protocols</b> that the application uses to communicate over the network.</p><ul><li><p><i>Common Layer 7 Protocols:</i> HTTP/HTTPS (for web browsing), DNS (for finding websites), SMTP (for email), WebSockets (for real-time chat).</p></li></ul><div style="color: white;"><br></div>What is it doing in System Design?<p>In System Design interviews, we rarely talk about Layers 1, 2, or 3 (cables, MAC addresses, and IP routing) because cloud providers (AWS, GCP) manage that for us.</p><p>However, we talk about <b>Layer 4 (Transport Layer - TCP/UDP)</b> and <b>Layer 7 (Application Layer - HTTP, WebSockets)</b> constantly.</p><p><br></p><p>When you design a system (like building Netflix or WhatsApp), you are designing the software that operates at Layer 7. You have to decide:</p><ul><li><p><i>How will my clients talk to my server?</i> (REST API over HTTP? Or real-time over WebSockets?)</p></li><li><p><i>How will I route traffic?</i> (An Application Load Balancer reading Layer 7 HTTP headers to send /video traffic to Server A and /images traffic to Server B).</p></li></ul><div style="color: white;"><br></div>Why do we separate it? (The "Three-Tier Architecture")<p>In System Design, you will often hear about separating the "Application Layer" (or Application Tier) from the rest of the system. In this context, "Application Layer" refers to your backend code (e.g., your Node.js, Python FastAPI, or Java Spring Boot servers).</p><p><br></p><p>We separate a modern system into three tiers:</p><ol><li><p><b>1.Presentation Tier (Frontend):</b> The React/Mobile app the user sees.</p></li><li><p><b>2.Application Tier (Backend logic):</b> The servers processing the rules, math, and logic.</p></li><li><p><b>3.Data Tier (Database):</b> PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Redis.</p></li></ol><p><b><br></b></p><p><b>Why separate the Application Layer from the Data Layer?</b></p><ul><li><p><b>Scalability:</b> If your web traffic spikes, your backend servers (Application Layer) might need to scale horizontally from 2 servers to 50 servers. You can easily clone Application Servers because they are <i>stateless</i> (they don''t store data permanently). You cannot easily clone databases.</p></li><li><p><b>Security:</b> Users on the internet should never be able to talk directly to your database. They talk to the Application Layer, which verifies their password, checks their permissions, and <i>then</i> the Application Layer talks to the database on their behalf.</p></li></ul><div style="color: white;"><br></div>Real-World Usecase &amp; Simple Demonstration<p>Let''s use <b>WhatsApp (Sending a message)</b> as a simple demonstration of how the OSI Application Layer (Layer 7) and the System Design Application Tier work together.</p><p><b>1. The User Action (The Trigger):</b></p><p>You type "Hello" in WhatsApp and hit send.</p><p><b>2. The OSI Application Layer (Layer 7 Protocol):</b></p><p>WhatsApp on your phone doesn''t just throw "Hello" into the air. It uses a Layer 7 protocol (like WebSockets or XMPP) to format a specific request:</p><p>{ action: "sendMessage", to: "User B", text: "Hello", token: "Auth123" }</p><p><b>3. The Transport (Layer 4):</b></p><p>That Layer 7 message is wrapped in a TCP connection and sent over the internet to WhatsApp''s servers.</p><p><b>4. The System Design Application Tier (Backend):</b></p><p>The request arrives at a Load Balancer, which forwards it to an <b>Application Server</b> (e.g., a Python or Erlang backend).</p><p>This server <i>is</i> your System Design Application Layer. It receives the Layer 7 message and runs the business logic:</p><ul><li><p><i>Is "Auth123" a valid token?</i> (Yes).</p></li><li><p><i>Is "User B" blocked by this user?</i> (No).</p></li></ul><p><b>5. The Data Tier:</b></p><p>The Application Server then tells the Database: <i>"Save this message to the history."</i></p><p><b>6. The Final Delivery:</b></p><p>The Application Server uses the Layer 7 protocol again to instantly push that "Hello" message to User B''s phone.</p><p><b>In Summary:</b> The Application Layer in the OSI model is the <i>language</i> your software uses to talk over the internet (HTTP). The Application Layer in System Design is the <i>backend server</i> that understands that language and executes your business rules. And keep everything intact</p></div>

</div>', '2026-07-30 11:58:12.972', '2026-08-02 09:14:00.009');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (18, 52, 'Notes', '<span style="font-weight: bold; text-decoration-line: underline;">Why CDN?</span>
<div style="color: white;">We use CDNs (Content Delivery Networks) to make websites load faster and crash less. Instead of serving every user from a single origin server, a CDN caches copies of a site''s files on a global network of "edge" servers, routing visitors to the geographically closest server to eliminate long delays. [1, 2, 3, 4]</div><div style="color: white;">A CDN is an essential tool for modern websites and applications because it delivers four primary benefits: [1]</div><div style="color: white;"></div><div style="color: white;"><div>Key Reasons to Use a CDN</div></div><ul style="color: white;"><div><li><strong>     Lightning-Fast Speed:</strong> By decreasing the physical distance data must travel, page load times are drastically reduced. [1, 2] </li></div><div><li><strong>     Origin Server Protection:</strong> CDNs absorb massive traffic spikes (like during a viral surge), ensuring your primary server never gets overwhelmed or crashes. [1, 2, 3]</li></div><div><li><strong>    Advanced Cyber Security:</strong> Leading CDNs protect websites from attacks by absorbing Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) traffic and using Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) to filter   out malicious bots</li></div></ul><div><span style="text-decoration-line: underline; font-weight: bold;"><br></span></div><div><span style="text-decoration-line: underline; font-weight: bold;">Definition</span></div><p style="color: white;">A content delivery network (CDN) is a globally distributed network of proxy servers, serving content from locations closer to the user. Generally, static files such as HTML/CSS/JS, photos, and videos are served from CDN, although some CDNs such as Amazon''s CloudFront support dynamic content. The site''s DNS resolution will tell clients which server to contact.</p><p style="color: white;">Serving content from CDNs can significantly improve performance in two ways:</p><ul style="color: white;"><li>-&gt;Users receive content from data centers close to them</li><li>-&gt;Your servers do not have to serve requests that the CDN fulfills</li></ul><div><span style="color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"><br></span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</span></div><div>
<p style="color: white;">Here are the two main architectural types of CDNs, followed by a few modern variations.</p><span style="color: white;">1. The Pull CDN (The "Lazy" Approach)</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>How it works:</strong> The CDN starts completely empty. When a user in London requests an image, the London Edge Server realizes it doesn''t have it (a "Cache Miss"). The Edge Server asks your main backend (the Origin Server) for the image, serves it to the user, and then <em>caches</em> (saves) a copy. The next person in London who asks for it gets it instantly (a "Cache Hit").</p></li><li><p><strong>When to use it:</strong> This is the most common type. It is best for heavy traffic websites with massive amounts of content where it would be impossible to push <em>everything</em> to every server in the world (e.g., Twitter, Instagram).</p></li><li><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Low maintenance. The CDN manages itself.</p></li><li><p><strong>Cons:</strong> The very first user to request a file experiences high latency because of the initial Cache Miss.</p></li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>2. The Push CDN (The "Eager" Approach)</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>How it works:</strong> As a developer, when you upload a file to your backend, your code actively pushes a copy of that file to all the CDN servers around the world <em>before</em> anyone even asks for it.</p></li><li><p><strong>When to use it:</strong> Best for a small amount of large, static files that rarely change but need to be instantly available everywhere (e.g., Game patches, a company''s whitepaper PDF, or a specific marketing video).</p></li><li><p><strong>Pros:</strong> 100% Cache Hit rate. The very first user gets the file instantly.</p></li><li><p><strong>Cons:</strong> Higher storage costs and more complex engineering, as you have to manage the uploading and syncing yourself.</p></li></ul><span style="color: white;">Modern CDN Categories (For Interview Bonus Points)</span><p style="color: white;">If you want to impress an interviewer, mention that modern CDNs do more than just cache static images.</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>3. Streaming / Video CDNs</strong>
Standard CDNs send files in one big chunk. Video CDNs are highly optimized to send data in continuous, tiny packets (like HLS or DASH protocols) so users can watch 4K video without buffering. (e.g., Netflix''s custom Open Connect network).</p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>4. Edge Computing CDNs (The Modern Wave)</strong>
Historically, CDNs only held "dumb" static files (images, CSS). Today, CDNs like Cloudflare or AWS CloudFront offer <strong>Edge Computing</strong> (e.g., Cloudflare Workers, Lambda@Edge).</p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><em>Why it matters:</em> You can deploy actual backend logic (like Node.js or Python functions) directly onto the CDN servers. This means you can authenticate users, modify HTTP headers, or do A/B testing at the edge, milliseconds away from the user, without ever hitting your main database!</p></li></ul><div><br></div></div><div>Main important Link Pull vs Push CDN: https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/pull-cdn-vs-push-cdn/</div><div><br></div><div><br></div>


', '2026-07-23 17:53:44.306', '2026-07-23 18:29:03.228');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (17, 51, 'linksss', 'https://github.com/donnemartin/system-design-primer#content-delivery-network<div><br></div><div>https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/how-to-crack-system-design-round-in-interviews/</div><div><br></div>', '2026-07-23 10:08:02.871', '2026-08-13 20:59:24.406');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (24, 56, 'System Integration', '<b style="color: white;">System integration</b><span style="color: white;">&nbsp;is defined in&nbsp;</span><span style="color: white;">engineering</span><span style="color: white;">&nbsp;as the process of bringing together the component sub-</span><span style="color: white;">systems</span><span style="color: white;">&nbsp;into one system (an aggregation of subsystems cooperating so that the system is able to deliver the overarching functionality) and ensuring that the subsystems function together as a system.</span>
<div><br></div><div><div style="color: white;">Methods of integration</div><p style="color: white;"><b>1.Vertical integration</b>&nbsp;(as opposed to "horizontal integration") is the process of integrating subsystems according to their functionality by creating functional entities also referred to as&nbsp;silos.[7]&nbsp;The benefit of this method is that the integration is performed quickly and involves only the necessary vendors, therefore, this method is cheaper in the short term. On the other hand, cost-of-ownership can be substantially higher than seen in other methods, since in case of new or enhanced functionality, the only possible way to implement (scale the system) would be by implementing another silo. Reusing subsystems to create another functionality is not possible.</p><p style="color: white;"><br></p><p style="color: white;"><b>2.Star integration</b>, also known as&nbsp;<b>spaghetti integration</b>, is a process of systems integration where each system is interconnected to each of the remaining subsystems. When observed from the perspective of the subsystem which is being integrated, the connections are reminiscent of a star, but when the overall diagram of the system is presented, the connections look like spaghetti, hence the name of this method. The cost varies because of the interfaces that subsystems are exporting. In a case where the subsystems are exporting heterogeneous or proprietary interfaces, the integration cost can substantially rise. Time and costs needed to integrate the systems increase exponentially when adding additional subsystems. From the feature perspective, this method often seems preferable, due to the extreme flexibility of the reuse of functionality.[8]</p><p style="color: white;"><b><br></b></p><p style="color: white;"><b>3.Horizontal integration</b>&nbsp;or&nbsp;<b>Enterprise Service Bus</b>&nbsp;(ESB) is an integration method in which a specialized subsystem is dedicated to communication between other subsystems. This allows cutting the number of connections (interfaces) to only one per subsystem which will connect directly to the ESB. The ESB is capable of translating the interface into another interface. This allows cutting the costs of integration and provides extreme flexibility.&nbsp;</p>

</div><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>', '2026-08-02 06:37:53.434', '2026-08-02 06:42:22.808');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (22, 55, 'PLAN', '<div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Yes — this is a very solid direction, and it is realistic.</div><div><br></div><div>The best way to make this feel like a real product is to build it in phases around the pain points you named.</div><div><br></div><div>## Recommended roadmap</div><div><br></div><div>### Part 1 — Project improvement phase</div><div>Focus here first because this improves the app immediately.</div><div><br></div><div>1. Notes section upgrade</div><div>- Replace the current basic note experience with a proper rich-text editor.</div><div>- Add:</div><div>  - font family</div><div>  - font size</div><div>  - bold / italic / underline</div><div>  - headings</div><div>  - bullet lists</div><div>  - quotes</div><div>  - links</div><div>  - code formatting</div><div>- Improve layout:</div><div>  - note list on one side</div><div>  - editor on the other</div><div>  - smoother switching between notes</div><div>  - autosave</div><div>  - better empty states</div><div>- Goal: make writing notes feel close to Microsoft Word or Notion, without being too heavy.</div><div><br></div><div>2. Auth and account setup</div><div>- Email/password signup and login</div><div>- Google login</div><div>- forgot password flow</div><div>- profile page</div><div>- account settings</div><div>- protected routes so each user only sees their own data</div><div>This will make the app feel complete and usable for real people.</div><div><br></div><div>---</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>### Part 2 — AI features integration</div><div>Keep these lightweight and optional so the user still controls the flow.</div><div><br></div><div>1. AI daily planner</div><div>- Generate a simple daily plan based on:</div><div>  - current goals</div><div>  - topics being studied</div><div>  - recent activity</div><div>  - streak or missed days</div><div>- The user can accept, edit, or ignore it.</div><div>- This should feel like a suggestion engine, not a rigid system.</div><div><br></div><div>2. AI summarizer for notes</div><div>- When a note is saved, AI can generate:</div><div>  - a short summary</div><div>  - key takeaways</div><div>  - revision bullets</div><div><br></div><div>3. Question generator</div><div>- Based on a topic note, AI can generate:</div><div>  - interview questions</div><div>  - practice questions</div><div>  - flashcard-style prompts</div><div>- This is very useful for prep and makes the app feel smarter.</div><div><br></div><div>These are the right AI features because they directly support your use case.</div><div><br></div><div>---</div><div><br></div><div>### Part 3 — Activity tracking and heatmap automation</div><div>This is the hardest part, but it is still possible.</div><div><br></div><div>The important point is this:</div><div>- Full automatic tracking is hard</div><div>- But “low-friction tracking” is very achievable</div><div><br></div><div>#### Best approach: make tracking feel automatic without being invasive</div><div><br></div><div>1. Add a simple session tracker</div><div>- Start a study session with one click</div><div>- Pause/stop when done</div><div>- Auto-log the session duration</div><div>- Auto-update the heatmap after the session ends</div><div><br></div><div>2. Add quick logging</div><div>- “Quick add study” button</div><div>- Pre-filled categories like DSA, System Design, AI, Projects</div><div>- One-tap logging for short sessions</div><div><br></div><div>3. Add a lightweight auto-detect mode</div><div>- If you want more automation later, detect:</div><div>  - active browser tab</div><div>  - coding editor usage</div><div>  - focus time</div><div>- Then classify it as study time and suggest a log entry</div><div><br></div><div>4. Use AI for classification</div><div>- After a session ends, AI can suggest:</div><div>  - topic</div><div>  - category</div><div>  - duration</div><div>  - summary</div><div>- The user can confirm or edit it</div><div><br></div><div>#### Realistic version</div><div>If you want something practical, I would recommend this order:</div><div><br></div><div>- Phase A: manual + timer-based logging</div><div>- Phase B: one-click session logging</div><div>- Phase C: browser-based passive tracking</div><div>- Phase D: deeper automation</div><div><br></div><div>That gives you a very smooth experience without making the app creepy or too complex.</div><div><br></div><div>---</div><div><br></div><div>## My recommended MVP order</div><div><br></div><div>If you want to ship something that feels polished, I would do it in this exact order:</div><div><br></div><div>1. Notes editor upgrade</div><div>2. Auth and account setup</div><div>3. Better dashboard and layout</div><div>4. AI daily planner</div><div>5. AI note summarizer and question generator</div><div>6. Study session tracker + heatmap updates</div><div><br></div><div>That is the sweet spot between useful and manageable.</div><div><br></div><div>## Short version</div><div>Your plan is good, and I would refine it like this:</div><div><br></div><div>- Make notes feel like a real writing tool</div><div>- Add proper auth and account experience</div><div>- Add AI that helps planning and revision</div><div>- Make activity tracking much easier with timers and quick logging</div><div>- Only then explore deeper automation</div><div><br></div><div>If you want, I can next turn this into a concrete implementation plan with:</div><div>- feature breakdown,</div><div>- frontend/backend tasks,</div><div>- and a milestone-based build order for the next 4–6 weeks.</div>', '2026-07-27 07:18:34.062', '2026-08-02 09:15:41.126');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (21, 53, 'LBA', '
<span style="color: white;">A load balancing algorithm is a method used by a load balancer to distribute incoming traffic and requests among multiple servers or resources. The primary purpose of a load balancing algorithm is to ensure efficient utilization of available resources, improve overall system performance, and maintain high availability and reliability.</span>
<div>
<span style="color: white;">1. Round Robin</span><p style="color: white;">This algorithm distributes incoming requests to servers in a cyclic order. It assigns a request to the first server, then moves to the second, third, and so on, and after reaching the last server, it starts again at the first.</p><p style="color: white;"><strong>Pros:</strong></p><ul style="color: white;"><li>Ensures an equal distribution of requests among the servers, as each server gets a turn in a fixed order.</li><li>Works well when servers have similar capacities.</li></ul><p style="color: white;"><strong>Cons:</strong></p><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>No Load Awareness</strong>: Does not take into account the current load or capacity of each server. All servers are treated equally regardless of their current state.</li><li><strong>No Session Affinity</strong>: Subsequent requests from the same client may be directed to different servers, which can be problematic for stateful applications.</li><li><strong>Performance Issues with Different Capacities</strong>: May not perform optimally when servers have different capacities or varying workloads.</li><li><strong>Predictable Distribution Pattern</strong>: Round Robin is predictable in its request distribution pattern, which could potentially be exploited by attackers who can observe traffic patterns and might find vulnerabilities in specific servers by predicting which server will handle their requests.</li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Use Cases</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Homogeneous Environments</strong>: Suitable for environments where all servers have similar capacity and performance.</li><li><strong>Stateless Applications</strong>: Works well for stateless applications where each request can be handled independently.</li></ul><div>
<br></div><div><span style="color: white;">2. Least Connections</span><p style="color: white;">The Least Connections algorithm is a dynamic load balancing technique that assigns incoming requests to the server with the fewest active connections at the time of the request. This method ensures a more balanced distribution of load across servers, especially in environments where traffic patterns are unpredictable and request processing times vary.</p><p style="color: white;"><strong>Pros:</strong></p><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Load Awareness</strong>: Takes into account the current load on each server by considering the number of active connections, leading to better utilization of server resources.</li><li><strong>Dynamic Distribution</strong>: Adapts to changing traffic patterns and server loads, ensuring no single server becomes a bottleneck.</li><li><strong>Efficiency in Heterogeneous Environments</strong>: Performs well when servers have varying capacities and workloads, as it dynamically allocates requests to less busy servers.</li></ul><p style="color: white;"><strong>Cons:</strong></p><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Higher Complexity</strong>: More complex to implement compared to simpler algorithms like Round Robin, as it requires real-time monitoring of active connections.</li><li><strong>State Maintenance</strong>: Requires the load balancer to maintain the state of active connections, which can increase overhead.</li><li><strong>Potential for Connection Spikes</strong>: In scenarios where connection duration is short, servers can experience rapid spikes in connection counts, leading to frequent rebalancing.</li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Use Cases</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Heterogeneous Environments</strong>: Suitable for environments where servers have different capacities and workloads, and the load needs to be dynamically distributed.</li><li><strong>Variable Traffic Patterns</strong>: Works well for applications with unpredictable or highly variable traffic patterns, ensuring that no single server is overwhelmed.</li><li><strong>Stateful Applications</strong>: Effective for applications where maintaining session state is important, as it helps distribute active sessions more evenly.</li></ul>
<span style="color: white;"><br></span></div><div><span style="color: white;">3. Weighted Round Robin</span><p style="color: white;">Weighted Round Robin (WRR) is an enhanced version of the Round Robin load balancing algorithm. It assigns weights to each server based on their capacity or performance, distributing incoming requests proportionally according to these weights. This ensures that more powerful servers handle a larger share of the load, while less powerful servers handle a smaller share.</p><span style="color: white; font-weight: bold;">Pros:</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Load Distribution According to Capacity</strong>: Servers with higher capacities handle more requests, leading to better utilization of resources.</li><li><strong>Flexibility</strong>: Easily adjustable to accommodate changes in server capacities or additions of new servers.</li><li><strong>Improved Performance</strong>: Helps in optimizing overall system performance by preventing overloading of less powerful servers.</li></ul><span style="color: white; font-weight: bold;">Cons:</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Complexity in Weight Assignment</strong>: Determining appropriate weights for each server can be challenging and requires accurate performance metrics.</li><li><strong>Increased Overhead</strong>: Managing and updating weights can introduce additional overhead, especially in dynamic environments where server performance fluctuates.</li><li><strong>Not Ideal for Highly Variable Loads</strong>: In environments with highly variable load patterns, WRR may not always provide optimal load balancing as it doesn''t consider real-time server load.</li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Use Cases</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Heterogeneous Server Environments</strong>: Ideal for environments where servers have different processing capabilities, ensuring efficient use of resources.</li><li><strong>Scalable Web Applications</strong>: Suitable for web applications where different servers may have varying performance characteristics.</li><li><strong>Database Clusters</strong>: Useful in database clusters where some nodes have higher processing power and can handle more queries.</li></ul><div><br></div><div><br></div>
<span style="color: white;">4. Weighted Least Connections</span><p style="color: white;">Weighted Least Connections is an advanced load balancing algorithm that combines the principles of the Least Connections and Weighted Round Robin algorithms. It takes into account both the current load (number of active connections) on each server and the relative capacity of each server (weight). This approach ensures that more powerful servers handle a proportionally larger share of the load, while also dynamically adjusting to the real-time load on each server.</p><span style="color: white;">Pros</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Dynamic Load Balancing</strong>: Adjusts to the real-time load on each server, ensuring a more balanced distribution of requests.</li><li><strong>Capacity Awareness</strong>: Takes into account the relative capacity of each server, leading to better utilization of resources.</li><li><strong>Flexibility</strong>: Can handle environments with heterogeneous servers and variable load patterns effectively.</li></ul><span style="color: white;">Cons</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Complexity</strong>: More complex to implement compared to simpler algorithms like Round Robin and Least Connections.</li><li><strong>State Maintenance</strong>: Requires the load balancer to keep track of both active connections and server weights, increasing overhead.</li><li><strong>Weight Assignment</strong>: Determining appropriate weights for each server can be challenging and requires accurate performance metrics.</li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Use Cases</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Heterogeneous Server Environments</strong>: Ideal for environments where servers have different processing capacities and workloads.</li><li><strong>High Traffic Web Applications</strong>: Suitable for web applications with variable traffic patterns, ensuring no single server becomes a bottleneck.</li><li><strong>Database Clusters</strong>: Useful in database clusters where nodes have varying performance capabilities and query loads.</li></ul>

<span style="color: white;">5. IP Hash</span><p style="color: white;">IP Hash load balancing is a technique that assigns client requests to servers based on the client''s IP address. The load balancer uses a hash function to convert the client''s IP address into a hash value, which is then used to determine which server should handle the request. This method ensures that requests from the same client IP address are consistently routed to the same server, providing session persistence.</p><span style="color: white;">Example</span><p style="color: white;">Suppose you have three servers (Server A, Server B, and Server C) and a client with the IP address&nbsp;192.168.1.10. The load balancer applies a hash function to this IP address, resulting in a hash value. If the hash value is 2 and there are three servers, the load balancer routes the request to Server C (2 % 3 = 2).</p><span style="color: white;">Pros</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Session Persistence</strong>: Ensures that requests from the same client IP address are consistently routed to the same server, which is beneficial for stateful applications.</li><li><strong>Simplicity</strong>: Easy to implement and does not require the load balancer to maintain the state of connections.</li><li><strong>Deterministic</strong>: Predictable and consistent routing based on the client''s IP address.</li></ul><span style="color: white;">Cons</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Uneven Distribution</strong>: If client IP addresses are not evenly distributed, some servers may receive more requests than others, leading to an uneven load.</li><li><strong>Dynamic Changes</strong>: Adding or removing servers can disrupt the hash mapping, causing some clients to be routed to different servers.</li><li><strong>Limited Flexibility</strong>: Does not take into account the current load or capacity of servers, which can lead to inefficiencies.</li></ul><span style="color: white;">Use Cases</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Stateful Applications</strong>: Ideal for applications where maintaining session persistence is important, such as online shopping carts or user sessions.</li><li><strong>Geographically Distributed Clients</strong>: Useful when clients are distributed across different regions and consistent routing is required.</li></ul>

<span style="color: white;">6. Least Response Time</span><p style="color: white;">Least Response Time load balancing is a dynamic algorithm that assigns incoming requests to the server with the lowest response time, ensuring efficient utilization of server resources and optimal client experience. This approach aims to direct traffic to the server that can handle the request the fastest, based on recent performance metrics.</p><span style="color: white;">How Least Response Time Load Balancing Works</span><ol style="color: white;"><li><strong>Monitor Response Times</strong>: The load balancer continuously monitors the response times of each server. Response time is typically measured from when a request is sent to a server until a response is received.</li><li><strong>Assign Requests</strong>: When a new request arrives, the load balancer assigns it to the server with the lowest average response time.</li><li><strong>Dynamic Adjustment</strong>: The load balancer dynamically adjusts the assignment of requests based on real-time performance data, ensuring that the fastest server handles the next request.</li></ol><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Pros</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Optimized Performance</strong>: Ensures that requests are handled by the fastest available server, leading to reduced latency and improved client experience.</li><li><strong>Dynamic Load Balancing</strong>: Continuously adjusts to changing server performance, ensuring optimal distribution of load.</li><li><strong>Effective Resource Utilization</strong>: Helps in better utilization of server resources by directing traffic to servers that can respond quickly.</li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Cons</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Complexity</strong>: More complex to implement compared to simpler algorithms like Round Robin, as it requires continuous monitoring of server performance.</li><li><strong>Overhead</strong>: Monitoring response times and dynamically adjusting the load can introduce additional overhead.</li><li><strong>Short-Term Variability</strong>: Response times can vary in the short term due to network fluctuations or transient server issues, potentially causing frequent rebalancing.</li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Use Cases</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Real-Time Applications</strong>: Ideal for applications where low latency and fast response times are critical, such as online gaming, video streaming, or financial trading platforms.</li><li><strong>Web Services</strong>: Useful for web services and APIs that need to provide quick responses to user requests.</li><li><strong>Dynamic Environments</strong>: Suitable for environments with fluctuating loads and varying server performance.</li></ul>
<br></div><div>
<span style="color: white;">7. Random</span><p style="color: white;">Random load balancing is a simple algorithm that distributes incoming requests to servers randomly. Instead of following a fixed sequence or using performance metrics, the load balancer selects a server at random to handle each request. This method can be effective in scenarios where the load is relatively uniform and servers have similar capacities.</p><p style="color: white;">Suppose you have three servers: Server A, Server B, and Server C. When a new request arrives, the load balancer randomly chooses one of these servers to handle the request. Over time, if the randomness is uniform, each server should receive approximately the same number of requests.</p><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Pros</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Simplicity</strong>: Very easy to implement and understand, requiring minimal configuration.</li><li><strong>No State Maintenance</strong>: The load balancer does not need to track the state or performance of servers, reducing overhead.</li><li><strong>Uniform Distribution Over Time</strong>: If the random selection is uniform, the load will be evenly distributed across servers over a long period.</li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div>Cons</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>No Load Awareness</strong>: Does not consider the current load or capacity of servers, which can lead to uneven distribution if server performance varies.</li><li><strong>Potential for Imbalance</strong>: In the short term, random selection can lead to an uneven distribution of requests.</li><li><strong>No Session Affinity</strong>: Requests from the same client may be directed to different servers, which can be problematic for stateful applications.</li><li>Security systems that rely on detecting anomalies (e.g., to mitigate DDoS attacks) might find it slightly more challenging to identify malicious patterns if a Random algorithm is used, due to the inherent unpredictability in request distribution. This could potentially dilute the visibility of attack patterns.</li></ul><span style="color: white;">Use Cases</span><ul style="color: white;"><li><strong>Homogeneous Environments</strong>: Suitable for environments where servers have similar capacity and performance.</li><li><strong>Stateless Applications</strong>: Works well for stateless applications where each request can be handled independently.</li><li><strong>Simple Deployments</strong>: Ideal for simple deployments where the complexity of other load balancing algorithms is not justified.</li></ul><div><font color="#ffffff"><br></font></div><div><font color="#ffffff"><br></font></div><div>
<div style="color: white;"><div>8. Least Bandwidth<p>The Least Bandwidth load balancing algorithm distributes incoming requests to servers based on the current bandwidth usage. It routes each new request to the server that is consuming the least amount of bandwidth at the time. This approach helps in balancing the network load more efficiently by ensuring that no single server gets overwhelmed with too much data traffic.</p><div><br></div>Pros<ul><li><strong>Dynamic Load Balancing</strong>: Continuously adjusts to the current network load, ensuring optimal distribution of traffic.</li><li><strong>Prevents Overloading</strong>: Helps in preventing any single server from being overwhelmed with too much data traffic, leading to better performance and stability.</li><li><strong>Efficient Resource Utilization</strong>: Ensures that all servers are utilized more effectively by balancing the bandwidth usage.</li></ul><div><br></div>Cons<ul><li><strong>Complexity</strong>: More complex to implement compared to simpler algorithms like Round Robin, as it requires continuous monitoring of bandwidth usage.</li><li><strong>Overhead</strong>: Monitoring bandwidth and dynamically adjusting the load can introduce additional overhead.</li><li><strong>Short-Term Variability</strong>: Bandwidth usage can fluctuate in the short term, potentially causing frequent rebalancing.</li></ul>Use Cases<ul><li><strong>High Bandwidth Applications</strong>: Ideal for applications with high bandwidth usage, such as video streaming, file downloads, and large data transfers.</li><li><strong>Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)</strong>: Useful for CDNs that need to balance traffic efficiently to deliver content quickly.</li><li><strong>Real-Time Applications</strong>: Suitable for real-time applications where maintaining low latency is critical.</li></ul></div></div><div style="color: white;"><div><div></div></div></div><br style="color: white;">
</div><div>
<div style="color: white;"><div><div><div>9. Custom Load<p>Custom Load load balancing is a flexible and highly configurable approach that allows you to define your own metrics and rules for distributing incoming traffic across a pool of servers. Unlike standard load balancing algorithms that use predefined criteria such as connection count or response time, Custom Load load balancing enables you to tailor the distribution strategy based on specific requirements and conditions unique to your application or infrastructure.</p>How Custom Load Load Balancing Works<ol><li><p><strong>Define Custom Metrics</strong>: Determine the metrics that best represent the load or performance characteristics relevant to your application. These metrics can include CPU usage, memory usage, disk I/O, application-specific metrics, or a combination of several metrics.</p></li><li><p><strong>Implement Monitoring</strong>: Continuously monitor the defined metrics on each server in the pool. This may involve integrating with monitoring tools or custom scripts that collect and report the necessary data.</p></li><li><p><strong>Create Load Balancing Rules</strong>: Establish rules and algorithms that use the monitored metrics to make load balancing decisions. This can be a simple weighted sum of metrics or more complex logic that prioritizes certain metrics over others.</p></li><li><p><strong>Dynamic Adjustment</strong>: Use the collected data and rules to dynamically adjust the distribution of incoming requests, ensuring that the traffic is balanced according to the custom load criteria.</p></li></ol><div><br></div>Pros<ul><li><strong>Flexibility</strong>: Allows for highly customized load balancing strategies tailored to the specific needs and performance characteristics of your application.</li><li><strong>Optimized Resource Utilization</strong>: Can lead to more efficient use of server resources by considering a comprehensive set of metrics.</li><li><strong>Adaptability</strong>: Easily adaptable to changing conditions and requirements, making it suitable for complex and dynamic environments.</li></ul><div><br></div>Cons<ul><li><strong>Complexity</strong>: More complex to implement and configure compared to standard load balancing algorithms.</li><li><strong>Monitoring Overhead</strong>: Requires continuous monitoring of multiple metrics, which can introduce additional overhead.</li><li><strong>Potential for Misconfiguration</strong>: Incorrectly defined metrics or rules can lead to suboptimal load balancing and performance issues.</li></ul><div><br></div>Use Cases<ul><li><strong>Complex Applications</strong>: Ideal for applications with complex performance characteristics and varying resource requirements.</li><li><strong>Highly Dynamic Environments</strong>: Suitable for environments where workloads and server performance can change rapidly and unpredictably.</li><li><strong>Custom Requirements</strong>: Useful when standard load balancing algorithms do not meet the specific needs of the application.</li></ul></div></div></div></div><div style="color: white;"><br></div></div>


</div>

</div>', '2026-07-26 18:50:08.916', '2026-07-29 07:09:10.472');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (20, 53, 'BASIC', '<span style="color: white; font-weight: bold; text-decoration-line: underline;">The Problem</span><p style="color: white;">You built a FastAPI application. It gets too popular, and your single server is maxing out its CPU.
You can''t just keep buying a bigger server forever (Vertical Scaling). Instead, you buy three cheaper servers and run your API on all of them (Horizontal Scaling).</p><p style="color: white;">But now you have a problem: When a user''s browser asks for your website, which of the three servers does it talk to?</p><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div><span style="font-weight: bold; text-decoration-line: underline;">The Solution: The Traffic Cop</span></span><p style="color: white;">A Load Balancer (LB) is a reverse proxy. It sits in front of your servers.
When a user types your URL, DNS actually resolves to the IP address of the <strong>Load Balancer</strong>, not your application servers.
The LB receives the request, decides which backend server is most available, forwards the request to it, and then passes the response back to the user.</p><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div><span style="font-style: italic;">1. The Most Important Question: L4 vs. L7</span></span><p style="color: white;"><span style="font-style: italic;">Interviewers love asking this. It refers to the OSI model layers. You must know the difference.</span></p><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>Layer 4 Load Balancing (Transport Layer)</strong></p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>How it works:</strong> It only looks at the IP address and the Port number (e.g., 192.168.1.5:80). It does not look at the actual contents of the request.</p></li><li><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Blazing fast. It doesn''t waste time decrypting SSL or reading headers. It just grabs the packet and throws it to a server.</p></li><li><p><strong>When to use:</strong> When you need raw speed and millions of connections per second (e.g., Multiplayer gaming, massive IoT data streams).</p></li><li><p><em>Example:</em> AWS Network Load Balancer (NLB).</p></li></ul><p style="color: white;"><strong><br></strong></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>Layer 7 Load Balancing (Application Layer)</strong></p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>How it works:</strong> It actually decrypts the HTTPS traffic and reads the HTTP request. It can see the URL path (/api/users), the headers, and the cookies.</p></li><li><p><strong>Pros:</strong> Highly intelligent routing. You can say: <em>"If the URL is /images, send it to Server A. If the URL is /video, send it to Server B."</em></p></li><li><p><strong>When to use:</strong> 95% of standard web applications and microservices.</p></li><li><p><em>Example:</em> AWS Application Load Balancer (ALB), Nginx.</p></li><li><p><br></p></li></ul><div><span style="color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/layer-4-load-balancing-vs-layer-7-load-balancing/</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"><br></span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(255, 255, 255);">Different types of LB (based on OSI layers) https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/system-design/layer-4l4-layer-7l7-and-gslb-load-balancers/</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(255, 255, 255);"><br></span></div><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div><span style="font-style: italic;">2. How does the LB choose a server? (Routing Algorithms)</span></span><p style="color: white;">When the LB gets a request, it has a menu of algorithms to choose who gets the work:</p><ul style="color: white;"><li><p><strong>-&gt;Round Robin:</strong> Passes requests sequentially. (Server A, then B, then C, then A...). Great if all your servers are identical and tasks take the same amount of time.</p></li><li><p><strong>-&gt;Weighted Round Robin:</strong> You can assign weights. (Server A is twice as powerful as Server B, so give A two requests for every one request B gets).</p></li><li><p><strong>-&gt;Least Connections:</strong> Sends the request to the server with the fewest active connections. Best if requests take varying amounts of time (e.g., long file uploads).</p></li><li><p><strong>-&gt;IP Hash:</strong> It does math on the user''s IP address to map them to a specific server. <em>Why?</em> Because it guarantees that User #123 will <strong>always</strong> be routed to Server B. This is crucial for "Sticky Sessions" (where a server keeps a user''s login state in its local RAM).</p></li></ul><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div><span style="font-style: italic;">3. How does it know a server is dead? (Health Checks)</span></span><p style="color: white;">If Server C unplugs, the LB shouldn''t send traffic to it.
To prevent this, the LB constantly sends "Health Checks" (heartbeats) to all servers—usually a quick GET request to a /health endpoint every 5 seconds.
If Server C fails to respond with an HTTP 200 OK three times in a row, the LB marks it as "Dead" and stops sending traffic there until it wakes back up.</p><span style="color: white;"><div><span style="color: white;"><br></span></div><span style="font-style: italic;">4. The Interview "Gotcha": The SPOF Trap</span></span><p style="color: white;"><strong>Interviewer:</strong> <em>"Okay, you put a load balancer in front of your 10 servers. But what happens if the Load Balancer itself crashes? Didn''t you just create a Single Point of Failure (SPOF)?"</em></p><p style="color: white;"><strong>The Answer:</strong>
You never have just one Load Balancer. You set them up in an <strong>Active-Passive</strong> pair.</p><ol style="color: white;"><li><p>You have LB 1 (Active) and LB 2 (Passive/Backup).</p></li><li><p>LB 1 receives all the traffic.</p></li><li><p>LB 2 just sits there and constantly "pings" LB 1.</p></li><li><p>Both LBs share a single <strong>Floating IP Address</strong> (or Virtual IP).</p></li><li><p>If LB 1 crashes, LB 2 stops receiving ping responses. LB 2 instantly takes ownership of the Floating IP. The users'' browsers don''t even notice the switch happened.</p></li></ol><p style="color: white;"><em>(Note: Tools like Keepalived manage this floating IP swap automatically).</em></p>

', '2026-07-26 10:15:49.595', '2026-07-30 05:11:42.092');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (26, 44, 'First Docker Container', '<h1 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Your First Docker Container</h1><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">To understand what you are about to do, remember this simple analogy:</p><ul style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><li><p><strong>The Dockerfile</strong> is the <strong>Blueprint</strong>.</p></li><li><p><strong>The Docker Image</strong> is the <strong>House</strong> built from the blueprint (static, nothing is happening inside yet).</p></li><li><p><strong>The Docker Container</strong> is when you turn on the lights and <strong>live in the house</strong> (the actively running code).</p></li></ul><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Step 1: Prepare Your Files</h3><ol style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><li><p>Create a brand new folder on your laptop (e.g., <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">my-docker-project</code>).</p></li><li><p>Inside that folder, save the <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">app.py</code> file.</p></li><li><p>Inside that exact same folder, save the <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Dockerfile</code>. <em>(Note: It must be named exactly <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Dockerfile</code> with a capital D and absolutely no file extension like .txt).</em></p></li></ol><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Step 2: Build the Image (Construct the House)</h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Open your terminal (Command Prompt, PowerShell, or Mac Terminal), navigate to the folder you just created, and run this command:</p><pre style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">docker build -t my-first-app .
</code></pre><ul style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><li><p><strong><code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">docker build</code></strong>: Tells Docker to read the blueprint.</p></li><li><p><strong><code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">-t my-first-app</code></strong>: Gives your new image a tag (a name) so you can find it later.</p></li><li><p><strong><code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">.</code> (The period)</strong>: This is crucial! It tells Docker to look in your <em>current</em> folder for the Dockerfile.</p></li></ul><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><em>Docker will now download a mini-Linux OS, install Python, copy your code, and save the Image to your hard drive.</em></p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><i>Here issues related to Dockerfile came up because windows sneaky activity of adding extension broke the rule of having a Dockerfile without any extension. So for this in cmd , manually I have to rename it (rename Dockerfile.txt Dockerfile)</i></p><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Step 3: Run the Container (Turn on the lights!)</h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Now that the image is built, let''s run it. Type this into your terminal:</p><pre style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">docker run my-first-app
</code></pre><ul style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><li><p><strong><code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">docker run</code></strong>: Tells Docker to take the static image and boot it up as a live container.</p></li></ul><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">If everything worked, you will instantly see the print statements from your Python code appear in your terminal! As soon as the Python script finishes printing, the container will automatically shut itself down.</p><h1 data-pm-slice="0 0 []">Your First Docker Container</h1><p>To understand what you are about to do, remember this simple analogy:</p><ul><li><p><strong>The Dockerfile</strong> is the <strong>Blueprint</strong>.</p></li><li><p><strong>The Docker Image</strong> is the <strong>House</strong> built from the blueprint (static, nothing is happening inside yet).</p></li><li><p><strong>The Docker Container</strong> is when you turn on the lights and <strong>live in the house</strong> (the actively running code).</p></li></ul><h3>Step 1: Prepare Your Files</h3><ol><li><p>Create a brand new folder on your laptop (e.g., <code>my-docker-project</code>).</p></li><li><p>Inside that folder, save the <code>app.py</code> file.</p></li><li><p>Inside that exact same folder, save the <code>Dockerfile</code>. <em>(Note: It must be named exactly <code>Dockerfile</code> with a capital D and absolutely no file extension like .txt).</em></p></li></ol><h3>Step 2: Build the Image (Construct the House)</h3><p>Open your terminal (Command Prompt, PowerShell, or Mac Terminal), navigate to the folder you just created, and run this command:</p><pre><code>docker build -t my-first-app .
</code></pre><ul><li><p><strong><code>docker build</code></strong>: Tells Docker to read the blueprint.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>-t my-first-app</code></strong>: Gives your new image a tag (a name) so you can find it later.</p></li><li><p><strong><code>.</code> (The period)</strong>: This is crucial! It tells Docker to look in your <em>current</em> folder for the Dockerfile.</p></li></ul><p><em>Docker will now download a mini-Linux OS, install Python, copy your code, and save the Image to your hard drive.</em></p><h3>Step 3: Run the Container (Turn on the lights!)</h3><p>Now that the image is built, let''s run it. Type this into your terminal:</p><pre><code>docker run my-first-app
</code></pre><ul><li><p><strong><code>docker run</code></strong>: Tells Docker to take the static image and boot it up as a live container.</p></li></ul><p>If everything worked, you will instantly see the print statements from your Python code appear in your terminal! As soon as the Python script finishes printing, the container will automatically shut itself down.</p>', '2026-08-04 11:52:38.659', '2026-08-04 11:55:02.418');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (25, 56, 'Monolith vs Microservice', '<h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Monolith Architecture</h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">With monolithic architectures, all processes are tightly coupled and run as a single service. This means that if one process of the application experiences a spike in demand, the entire architecture must be scaled. Adding or improving a monolithic application’s features becomes more complex as the code base grows. This complexity limits experimentation and makes it difficult to implement new ideas. Monolithic architectures add risk for application availability because many dependent and tightly coupled processes increase the impact of a single process failure.</p><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Microservice Architecture</h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">With a microservices architecture, an application is built as independent components that run each application process as a service. These services communicate via a well-defined interface using lightweight APIs. Services are built for business capabilities and each service performs a single function. Because they are independently run, each service can be updated, deployed, and scaled to meet demand for specific functions of an application.</p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><img src="https://i.ibb.co/jPdqyPQX/image.png" style="border-radius: 0.375rem; margin: 0.5rem 0px;"></p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><br></p><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">The Concept: Monolith vs. Microservices</h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">To understand Microservices, you must first understand what it replaces: <strong>The Monolith</strong>.</p><ul style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><li><p><strong>The Monolith:</strong> Imagine you are building an e-commerce app (like Amazon). In a monolithic architecture, your User Authentication, Product Catalog, Shopping Cart, and Payment Processing are all written in the exact same codebase, running on the exact same server, and connecting to the exact same database.</p><ul><li><p><em>Analogy:</em> A restaurant where one single person acts as the host, the chef, the waiter, and the cashier. If they get overwhelmed taking orders, the cooking completely stops.</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Microservices:</strong> You take that massive codebase and break it into separate, independent mini-applications (services). You have an Auth Service, a Catalog Service, a Cart Service, and a Payment Service. Each service runs on its own server (or container) and usually has its own dedicated database. They talk to each other over the network, typically using REST APIs or Message Queues.</p><ul><li><p><em>Analogy:</em> A modern restaurant kitchen. You have a dedicated host, a dedicated grill chef, a dedicated salad chef, and a dedicated cashier. They communicate by shouting ticket orders (APIs).</p></li></ul></li></ul><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Why do companies use them? (The Pros)</h3><ol style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><li><p><strong>Independent Scaling:</strong> If it''s Black Friday, millions of people are browsing products, but only a few are buying. In a Monolith, you have to duplicate the <em>entire</em> massive application across 100 servers to handle the traffic. With Microservices, you only scale up the "Catalog Service" to 100 servers, while leaving the "Payment Service" running on just 5 servers. This saves a massive amount of money.</p></li><li><p><strong>Independent Deployments:</strong> If a developer makes a typo in the Shopping Cart code, only the Cart Service crashes. Users can still browse products and read reviews. In a Monolith, that same typo takes down the entire website.</p></li><li><p><strong>Polyglot Programming:</strong> The AI team can write the Recommendation Service in Python, the backend team can write the Payment Service in Java, and the frontend team can write the UI Service in Node.js. They just communicate via standard HTTP/JSON, so the language doesn''t matter.</p></li></ol><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">The Catch (The Cons)</h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Microservices solve organizational and scaling problems, but they introduce massive <strong>Networking and Complexity problems</strong>.</p><ol style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><li><p><strong>Network Latency &amp; Failures:</strong> In a Monolith, the Cart calling the Payment system takes 1 millisecond (it''s just a local function call in RAM). In Microservices, that call has to travel over physical network cables. What if the network drops the packet? You now have to write complex retry logic and handle timeouts.</p></li><li><p><strong>Data Consistency:</strong> If the Payment Service successfully charges a credit card, but the Order Service crashes before saving the receipt, your system is in a broken state. Managing distributed transactions across multiple databases is notoriously difficult.</p></li><li><p><strong>Operational Overhead:</strong> You now have to monitor, deploy, and manage logs for 50 different applications instead of just 1. This requires advanced DevOps tools like Kubernetes and Docker.</p></li></ol><blockquote style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">How to discuss this in an interview (1 YOE)</blockquote><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">They expect you to know </span><strong style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">the tradeoffs</strong><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">. If an interviewer asks, </span><em style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">"Should we build our new startup''s app using Microservices?"</em><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">
The best answer is often </span><strong style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">No</strong><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">. You start with a Monolith to move fast and find product-market fit. You only split into Microservices when your engineering team gets too large to work in one codebase, or a specific feature requires independent scaling.</span></p>', '2026-08-02 11:16:33.08', '2026-08-03 21:10:25.645');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (27, 57, 'SQL vs NoSQL', '<h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">SQL (Relational Databases)</h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">SQL databases store data in highly structured, rigid <b>tables</b> with rows and columns (like Excel spreadsheets).</p><ul style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><li><p><b>The Rule:</b> The structure (schema) is strictly defined upfront. If a table expects a "Date of Birth" column, every single row must either have a date or explicitly be left empty (NULL); you cannot suddenly insert an array of hobbies into that column.</p></li><li><p><b>Best For:</b> Complex queries, financial transactions, and highly related data where data integrity (ACID compliance) is the absolute highest priority.</p></li><li><p><i>Examples:</i> PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle.</p><h3 data-path-to-node="1">SQL (Relational Databases)</h3><p data-path-to-node="2">SQL databases store data in highly structured, rigid <b data-path-to-node="2" data-index-in-node="53">tables</b> with rows and columns (like Excel spreadsheets).</p></li><ul data-path-to-node="3"><li><p data-path-to-node="3,0,0"><b data-path-to-node="3,0,0" data-index-in-node="0">The Rule:</b> The structure (schema) is strictly defined upfront. If a table expects a "Date of Birth" column, every single row must either have a date or explicitly be left empty (NULL); you cannot suddenly insert an array of hobbies into that column.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="3,1,0"><b data-path-to-node="3,1,0" data-index-in-node="0">Best For:</b> Complex queries, financial transactions, and highly related data where data integrity (ACID compliance) is the absolute highest priority.</p></li><li><p data-path-to-node="3,2,0"><i data-path-to-node="3,2,0" data-index-in-node="0">Examples:</i> PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle</p></li></ul></ul><div><font color="#f1f5f9"><br></font></div><div><a style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">NoSQL</a><span style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">&nbsp;is a non-relational database, meaning it allows different structures than a SQL database (not rows and columns) and more flexibility to use a format that best fits the data. The term “NoSQL” was not coined until the early 2000s. It doesn’t mean the systems don’t use SQL, as NoSQL databases do sometimes support some SQL commands. More accurately, “NoSQL” is sometimes defined as “not only SQL.”</span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><br></span></div><div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><h2>How SQL works</h2></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><div><p>SQL databases are valuable in handling structured data, or data that has relationships between its variables and entities.</p></div></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Scalability</h3></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><div><p>In general, SQL databases can scale vertically, meaning you can increase the load on a server by migrating to a larger server that adds more CPU, RAM or&nbsp;<a style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">SSD</a>&nbsp;capability. While vertical scalability is used most frequently, SQL databases can also scale horizontally through sharding or partitioning logic, although that’s not well-supported.</p></div></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Structure</h3></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><div><p>SQL database schema organizes data in relational, tabular ways, using tables with columns or attributes and rows of records. Because SQL works with such a strictly predefined schema, it requires organizing and structuring data before starting with the SQL database.</p></div></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">Properties</h3></div><div style=""><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><p>RDBMS, which use SQL, must exhibit four properties, known by the acronym ACID. These ensure that transactions are processed successfully and that the SQL database has a high level of reliability:</p><ul><li>Atomicity:&nbsp;All transactions must succeed or fail completely and cannot be left partially complete, even in the case of system failure.</li><li>Consistency:&nbsp;The database must follow rules that validate and prevent corruption at every step.</li><li>Isolation:&nbsp;Concurrent transactions cannot affect each other.</li><li>Durability:&nbsp;Transactions are final, and even system failure cannot “roll back” a complete transaction.</li></ul><div><font size="4"><br></font></div></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><font size="4">How SQL works</font></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">SQL databases are valuable in handling structured data, or data that has relationships between its variables and entities.</div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><br></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><u>Scalability</u></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">In general, SQL databases can scale vertically, meaning you can increase the load on a server by migrating to a larger server that adds more CPU, RAM or SSD capability. While vertical scalability is used most frequently, SQL databases can also scale horizontally through sharding or partitioning logic, although that’s not well-supported.</div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><u><br></u></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><u>Structure</u></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">SQL database schema organizes data in relational, tabular ways, using tables with columns or attributes and rows of records. Because SQL works with such a strictly predefined schema, it requires organizing and structuring data before starting with the SQL database.</div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><u><br></u></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><u>Properties</u></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">RDBMS, which use SQL, must exhibit four properties, known by the acronym ACID. These ensure that transactions are processed successfully and that the SQL database has a high level of reliability:</div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><br></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249);"><ul><li>Atomicity: All transactions must succeed or fail completely and cannot be left partially complete, even in the case of system failure.</li><li>Consistency: The database must follow rules that validate and prevent corruption at every step.</li><li>Isolation: Concurrent transactions cannot affect each other.</li><li><font color="#f1f5f9">Durability: Transactions are final, and even system failure cannot “roll back” a complete transaction.How SQL works</font></li><li><font color="#f1f5f9">SQL databases are valuable in handling structured data, or data that has relationships between its variables and entities.</font></li></ul></div><blockquote style="">Examples: Db2, MySQL, POSTGRE SQL, COCKROACH DB, AZURE SQL DB, ORACLE DB&nbsp;</blockquote><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><h2 style="background: transparent !important;">How NoSQL works</h2></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;">Unlike SQL, NoSQL systems allow you to work with different data structures within a database. Because they allow a dynamic schema for unstructured data, there’s less need to pre-plan and pre-organize data, and it’s easier to make modifications. NoSQL databases allow you to add new attributes and fields, as well as use varied syntax across databases.</p></div></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">Scalability</h3></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;">NoSQL databases scale better horizontally, which means one can add additional servers or nodes as needed to increase load.</p></div></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">Structure</h3></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;">NoSQL databases are not relational, so they don’t solely store data in rows and tables. Instead, they generally fall into one of four types of structures:</p><ul style="background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;">Column-oriented,&nbsp;where data is stored in cells grouped in a virtually unlimited number of columns rather than rows.</li><li style="background: transparent !important;">Key-value stores, which use an associative array (also known as a dictionary or map) as their data model. This model represents data as a collection of key-value pairs.</li><li style="background: transparent !important;">Document stores, which use documents to hold and encode data in standard formats, including XML, YAML, JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) and BSON. A benefit is that documents within a single database can have different data types.</li><li style="background: transparent !important;">Graph databases, which represent data on a graph that shows how different sets of data relate to each other. Neo4j, RedisGraph (a graph module built into Redis) and OrientDB are examples of graph databases.</li></ul></div></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">Properties</h3></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;">While SQL calls for ACID properties, NoSQL follows the CAP theory (although some NoSQL databases — such as IBM’s DB2, MongoDB, AWS’s DynamoDB and Apache’s CouchDB — can also integrate and follow ACID rules).</p><p style="background: transparent !important;">The&nbsp;<a style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">CAP theorem</a>&nbsp;says that distributed data systems allow a trade-off that can guarantee only two of the following three properties (which form the acronym CAP) at any one time:</p><ul style="background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;">Consistency:&nbsp;Every request receives either the most recent result or an error. MongoDB is an example of a strongly consistent system, whereas others such as Cassandra offer eventual consistency.</li><li style="background: transparent !important;">Availability:&nbsp;Every request has a non-error result.</li><li style="background: transparent !important;">Partition tolerance:&nbsp;Any delays or losses between nodes do not interrupt the system operation.</li></ul></div></div><div style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><blockquote style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">Examples: MongoDB, Cassandra, CouchDB, Redis</blockquote><div style="background: transparent !important;"><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">When to use SQL</h3></div><div style="background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;">SQL is a good choice when working with related data. Relational databases are efficient, flexible and easily accessed by any application. A benefit of a relational database is that when one user updates a specific record, every instance of the database automatically refreshes, and that information is provided in real-time.</p><p style="background: transparent !important;">SQL databases are the gold standard for handling massive amounts of structured information efficiently.</p><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">The Core Advantages:</b></p><ol style="background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Normalization:</b> You only have to update a piece of data in <i style="background: transparent !important;">one</i> place, completely eliminating the confusion of conflicting versions across multiple files.</p></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Data Integrity:</b> Because of strict rules (ACID compliance), you can trust that your data is always accurate, consistent, and safe from partial failures.</p></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Scalability:</b> They are built to securely scale up as your business grows. (Most cases it''s vertical)</p></li></ol><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Who uses it?</b>
Almost everyone. Despite building their own custom database systems, tech giants like <b style="background: transparent !important;">Google, Amazon, Facebook, Netflix, Uber, and Airbnb</b> still rely heavily on SQL to query and analyze their core data.</p><div style="background: transparent !important;"><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">When to use NoSQL</h3></div><div style="background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><strong style="background: transparent !important;"></strong>While SQL is valued for ensuring data validity, NoSQL is good when it’s more important that the availability of big data is fast. It’s also a good choice when a company will need to scale because of changing requirements. NoSQL is easy-to-use, flexible and offers high performance.</p><p style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Scalability is a significant benefit of NoSQL databases. Unlike with SQL, their built-in sharding and high availability requirements allow horizontal scaling. Furthermore, NoSQL databases like Cassandra, developed by Facebook, handle massive amounts of data spread across many servers, having no single points of failure and providing maximum availability. <b>Amazon, Netflix, Google, ebay are companies using NoSQL.</b></span></p><p style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;"><b><br></b></span></p><p style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;"><b>---------------------------------------------------End--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</b></span></p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div>', '2026-08-08 06:26:33.43', '2026-08-08 07:00:43.46');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (29, 59, 'Multi-Tenancy Architecture', '<span style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">Multi-tenancy architecture is a<span style="background: transparent !important;">&nbsp;</span></span><a style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">system design</span></a><span style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">&nbsp;</span>where a single instance of software serves multiple customers, known as tenants. Each tenant''s data is isolated and invisible to others, ensuring privacy and security. This approach is efficient because it allows resource sharing, reduces costs, and simplifies updates. Tenants can customize their experience without affecting others. Multi-tenancy is commonly used in cloud services, where different users or organizations share the same infrastructure but operate independently.</span><div><span style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><br></span></div><div><span style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><h2 style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Types of Multi-Tenancy Architectures</span></h2><p style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Multi-tenancy architectures in system design are varied, each offering distinct ways to manage multiple tenants while balancing factors like resource sharing, isolation, and complexity. The primary types include:</span></p><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">1. Isolated Tenancy</span></h3><p style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Each tenant has their own separate instance of the application and database. This approach provides the highest level of isolation and security, making it ideal for applications requiring strict data segregation and compliance with stringent regulations.&nbsp;</span></p></span><ul style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Superior data isolation and security, straightforward compliance with regulatory requirements.</span></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">High operational costs due to redundant resource allocation, less efficient use of resources, and challenges in scalability and maintenance</span></li></ul><div><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">2.Shared Application with Separate Databases</span></h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Multiple tenants share the same application instance, but each has a separate database. This model strikes a balance between resource utilization and data isolation, offering efficient use of application resources while maintaining tenant-specific databases.</span></p><ul style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Better resource utilization compared to isolated tenancy, maintained data isolation at the database level, simpler application management.</span></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Increased complexity in managing multiple databases, potential challenges in scaling the database layer independently.</span></li></ul><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">3. Shared Application and Shared Database with Separate Schemas</span></h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Tenants share the same database, but each tenant''s data is stored in a separate schema. This method provides efficient database resource use while maintaining some level of data segregation.</span></p><ul style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Efficient database resource utilization, simplified application deployment and management, reasonable data isolation.</span></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Less isolation compared to separate databases, potential for schema conflicts, increased complexity in schema management and migration.</span></li></ul><div><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">4. Shared Everything</span></h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Tenants share the same application instance and the same database tables. This model maximizes resource utilization and simplifies deployment and scaling, as all tenants operate within the same environment.</span></p><ul style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Maximum resource utilization, simplified deployment and scaling processes, lower operational costs.</span></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Complex data management to ensure tenant data isolation, higher risk of data leakage</span></li></ul><div><h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">5. Hybrid Multi-Tenancy</span></h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Combines elements from different multi-tenancy models to balance resource efficiency and data isolation based on specific needs. This approach allows flexibility in choosing the best aspects of each model to optimize performance and cost.</span></p><ul style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Highly adaptable and flexible, can be tailored to meet specific requirements of isolation, performance, and cost-efficiency.</span></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">Complex to implement both mechanism wise as well cost wise.</span></li></ul><div><br></div></div></div></div><blockquote><font size="4">What does actually same pr different instance means?</font></blockquote><p><br></p><p><img src="https://i.ibb.co/mrgfXgZH/image.png" style="border-radius: 0.375rem; margin: 0.5rem 0px;"></p>For Isolated tenancy, real world example:&nbsp;<b style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">Atlassian Isolated Cloud</b> or <b style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">Managed Hosting for Banks</b>. If a highly regulated healthcare network or a government defense contractor wants to use Jira, they legally cannot have their data sitting on the same database as a random gaming startup. <span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">Atlassian spins up dedicated compute, storage, and networking resources </span><i style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">just</span></i><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"> for that one organization.</span></div><div><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><br></span></div><div><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;"><div style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;">Examples for each of the 5 multi-tenancy architecture types:</p><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">1. Isolated Tenancy (Single-Tenant / Siloed)</b></p><ul style="background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Example:</b> High-security enterprise software like <b style="background: transparent !important;">SAP S/4HANA Cloud (Private Edition)</b> or dedicated <b style="background: transparent !important;">AWS GovCloud</b> deployments.</p></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">How it works:</b> A government agency or major bank gets their own completely separate application instance and dedicated database server. No other company''s software or data touches that hardware.</p></li></ul><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">2. Shared Application with Separate Databases</b></p><ul style="background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Example:</b> <b style="background: transparent !important;">Shopify</b> (or traditional multi-tenant CRM setups).</p></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">How it works:</b> Every store owner uses the exact same core Shopify web application code and features, but Store A''s orders and customers live in Database A, while Store B''s data lives in Database B.</p></li></ul><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">3. Shared Application &amp; Shared Database with Separate Schemas</b></p><ul style="background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Example:</b> Cloud database services like <b style="background: transparent !important;">PostgreSQL hosted on AWS RDS for B2B SaaS</b>.</p></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">How it works:</b> Multiple client companies share a single PostgreSQL database instance, but Client A''s tables live in <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">schema_tenant_a</code> while Client B''s live in <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">schema_tenant_b</code>. They share the database engine and hardware, but logical schema boundaries prevent table collisions.</p></li></ul><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">4. Shared Everything (Shared App &amp; Shared Database Tables)</b></p><ul style="background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Example:</b> Standard modern B2B SaaS applications like <b style="background: transparent !important;">Slack</b> or <b style="background: transparent !important;">Zendesk</b>.</p></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">How it works:</b> All companies (Tenant A, Tenant B) use the same application and store data in the same database tables (e.g., a single <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">Users</code> or <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">Messages</code> table). Every SQL query relies on a mandatory filter column (like <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">WHERE tenant_id = ''company_123''</code>) to keep data isolated.</p></li></ul><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">5. Hybrid Multi-Tenancy</b></p><ul style="background: transparent !important;"><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Example:</b> <b style="background: transparent !important;">Salesforce</b> or <b style="background: transparent !important;">Workday</b>.</p></li><li style="background: transparent !important;"><p style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">How it works:</b> Standard, low-tier business clients are placed on a cheap "Shared Everything" or "Shared Schema" setup. However, if a massive enterprise customer signs up and demands strict isolation, the system provisions a dedicated database or separate instance just for them, while keeping them on the same platform management umbrella.</p></li></ul></div></span></div></span></div><span style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;"></span></span></span><span style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"> </span>', '2026-08-12 10:26:05.899', '2026-08-12 10:41:53.985');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (28, 58, 'Basics', 'LL is a linear data structure where each item (node) consists of two things , first is the data and second is the reference (pointer) to next data.&nbsp;<div><br></div><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">We have created a Node class in which we have defined a __init__ function to initialize the node with the data passed as an argument and a reference with None because if we have only one node then there is nothing in its reference.</span></p><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>class Node:

&nbsp; &nbsp; def __init__(self, data):

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; self.data = data

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; self.next = None

-------------------------------------------------------<br># Create nodes<br>node1 = Node(15)<br>node2 = Node(3)<br>node3 = Node(17)<br>node4 = Node(90)<br><br># Link nodes<br>node1.next = node2<br>node2.next = node3<br>node3.next = node4  <br><br>head = node1  # Head points to the first node<br><br># Traverse and print the linked list<br>current = head<br>while current:<br>    print(current.data, end=" -&gt; ")<br>    current = current.next<br>print("None")</code></pre><p><font size="5"><br></font></p><p><font size="5">Basic Operations:<br></font><b>1. Traversal</b></p><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>current=head 
while current is not None:

&nbsp; &nbsp; print(current.data,end="")

&nbsp; &nbsp; if(current.next is not None):

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; print("-&gt;",end="")

&nbsp; &nbsp; current=current.next</code></pre><p><b>2. Insertion</b></p><p>2.1 At beginning : make a new node and attach it to previous head</p><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>&nbsp; &nbsp; newhead=Node(x)

&nbsp; &nbsp; newhead.next=head</code></pre><p>2.2 At&nbsp; any specific index</p><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>def insertpos(head,pos,val):

&nbsp; &nbsp; current=head

&nbsp; &nbsp; while(pos-1&gt;0):

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; print(current.data," ",end="")

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; pos=pos-1

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; current=current.next &nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;

&nbsp; &nbsp; newnode=Node(val)

&nbsp; &nbsp; newnode.next=current.next

&nbsp; &nbsp; current.next=newnode</code></pre><p>&nbsp;2.3 At end&nbsp;</p><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>lasthead=Node(x)

curr=head

while(curr.next is not None):

&nbsp; &nbsp; curr=curr.next

curr.next=lasthead</code></pre><p><b><br></b></p><p><b>3. Deletion</b></p><p><b>3.1&nbsp;</b>&nbsp;At beginning</p><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>temp=newhead

newhead=newhead.next

temp=None

printll(newhead)</code></pre><p>3.2 At last</p><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>curr=newhead

while(curr.next.next is not None):  # go to second last node

&nbsp; &nbsp; curr=curr.next

curr.next=None

print("")

printll(newhead)</code></pre><p>3.3 At any specific position</p><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>def delbypos(head,x):

&nbsp; &nbsp; curr=head

&nbsp; &nbsp; t=1

&nbsp; &nbsp; while t&lt;x-1:   # to reach the node just before x position

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; curr=curr.next

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; t=t+1

&nbsp; &nbsp; curr.next=curr.next.next    #the node at x is unlinked from the list

delbypos(head,3)</code></pre><p>4. Searching a target value&nbsp;</p><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>def search(head,target):

&nbsp; &nbsp; curr=head

&nbsp; &nbsp; while(curr is not None):

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; if(curr.data==target):

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; print("Found!",curr.data)

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; return

&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; curr=curr.next

&nbsp; &nbsp; print("Not found!")

&nbsp; &nbsp; return</code></pre><p>5. Modifying a linked list item is all about accessing the node and just directly changing the value like&nbsp;<span style="background-color: rgb(30, 41, 59); color: rgb(244, 63, 94); font-family: &quot;IBM Plex Mono&quot;, &quot;JetBrains Mono&quot;, ui-monospace, monospace; font-size: 0.85em;">head.data=any value</span></p><p>6. Reverse a linked list</p><p>It''s basically the swapping of two node''s direction.&nbsp; For a current node, first we store the next node (current.next in a variable), then the current.next is just pointed to previous node. Done, and hence in each step you move the prev and current, by setting prev=current and current=the variable (which stores next element)</p><pre class="bg-slate-950/80 p-3 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs my-2 overflow-x-auto text-emerald-400 block"><code>prev=None
curr=head
while curr:
&nbsp; &nbsp; nextNode=curr.next
&nbsp; &nbsp; curr.next=prev
&nbsp; &nbsp; prev=curr
&nbsp; &nbsp; curr=nextNode</code></pre><p><br></p><p><br></p>', '2026-08-08 09:09:03.06', '2026-08-13 17:14:18.449');
INSERT INTO public."TopicNote" (id, "topicId", title, content, "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES (30, 44, 'Docker Commands', '<h3 style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">What actually is a "Pull"?</h3><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">In Docker, a <b style="background: transparent !important;">pull</b> is simply the act of downloading a Docker Image (the blueprint) from a remote registry (like Docker Hub) down to your local computer or server.&nbsp;</p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><font face="Inter, sans-serif"><b>1. docker run &lt; ... &gt;&nbsp; runs the container from the image</b></font></p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><b style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; background: transparent !important;">You run a container:</b><span style="color: oklch(0.968 0.007 247.896); font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;"> If you type </span><code style="background: transparent !important; color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">docker run ubuntu</code><span style="color: oklch(0.968 0.007 247.896); font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;"> and you don''t already have the Ubuntu image saved on your hard drive, Docker automatically pulls it first from docker hub</span></p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><i><span style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Anonymous (Not logged in)-&gt;&nbsp;</b></span><span style="background: transparent !important;">100 pulls per 6 hours :&nbsp;</span><span style="background: transparent !important;">Tracked by your computer''s IP address.</span></i></p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><i><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Logged in(Free tier)-&gt; 2</b></span><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; background: transparent !important;">00 pulls per 6 hours :&nbsp;</span><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; background: transparent !important;">Tracked by Docker Id and Username</span></i></p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; background: transparent !important;"><i><span style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Pro / Team / Business -&gt;&nbsp;</b></span><span style="background: transparent !important;"><b style="background: transparent !important;">Unlimited&nbsp; &nbsp;:</b></span><span style="background: transparent !important;">Tracked by your Docker ID username.</span></i></span></p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">-&gt; docker pull ubuntu will only download the image , will not run it</p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;"><br></span></span></p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><span style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">2.&nbsp;</span></span><span style="color: oklch(0.968 0.007 247.896); font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">The </span><code style="background: transparent !important; color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">docker ps</code><span style="color: oklch(0.968 0.007 247.896); font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;"> command (short for process status) is used to list your Docker containers. By default, running it without any options will only show containers that are currently </span><b style="font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif; background: transparent !important;">running</b><span style="color: oklch(0.968 0.007 247.896); font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">.</span></p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><img src="https://i.ibb.co/DgSwTXZD/image.png" style="border-radius: 0.375rem; margin: 0.5rem 0px;"></p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;"><br></p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">3. docker stop command is used to stop the container using it''s name or id.&nbsp;<code style="background: transparent !important; color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">docker stop my-python-container</code><span style="color: oklch(0.968 0.007 247.896); font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;"> or </span><code style="background: transparent !important; color: rgb(241, 245, 249);">docker stop 1a2b3c4d5e</code><span style="color: oklch(0.968 0.007 247.896); font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;">. You can also stop multiple containers simultaneously by listing their names or IDs separated by spaces.</span></p><blockquote style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">&nbsp;From a technical interview perspective, the key detail is <i style="background: transparent !important;">how</i> it works under the hood. The command first sends a <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">SIGTERM</code> signal to the main process inside the container, giving your application a chance to save data, finish requests, and close connections safely. If the process does not exit within the default grace period of 10 seconds, Docker forcefully terminates it by issuing a <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">SIGKILL</code> signal (which is exactly what the <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">docker kill</code> command does instantly).&nbsp;&nbsp;</blockquote><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">-&gt; docker rm &lt;container name&gt; removes the container</p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">&nbsp;Before deleting a container with <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">docker rm</code>, you must ensure it is <b style="background: transparent !important;">stopped</b> (using <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">docker stop</code>), as Docker prevents the removal of running containers unless you force it with <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">-f</code>. More importantly, you must verify that any critical data generated inside that container is safely stored in a <b style="background: transparent !important;">Docker Volume</b> or bind mount; otherwise, deleting the container will permanently wipe out that data.</p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">-&gt; docker rmi &lt;image name&gt;:&nbsp; this&nbsp;<span style="background: transparent !important;">command is </span><span style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">used to </span><strong style="background: transparent !important;"><span style="background: transparent !important;">remove (delete) one or more local Docker images</span></strong><span style="background: transparent !important;"> from your host node</span></span><span style="background: transparent !important;">. It is a direct alias for the&nbsp;</span><span style="background: transparent !important;"><code class="bg-slate-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded border border-slate-700 font-mono text-xs text-rose-400">docker image rm</code>&nbsp;command.</span><span style="background: transparent !important;"> </span>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">&nbsp;Before deleting an image with <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">docker rmi</code>, you must <b style="background: transparent !important;">delete all containers</b> (even stopped ones) that were built from it. Docker''s safety mechanism locks the image and throws a conflict error if any container relies on it as its base layer. You must run <code style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">docker rm</code> on those dependent containers first before the image can be successfully removed.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p style="color: rgb(241, 245, 249); background: transparent !important;">4. docker images (TO see all the images we have with their basic details)&nbsp;</p>', '2026-08-14 06:37:10.755', '2026-08-14 07:15:16.374');


